MMAACCRROO RREEFFEERREENNCCEE
     This section is a canonical reference to all macros, arranged alphabeti‐
     cally.  For the scoping of individual macros, see _M_A_C_R_O _S_Y_N_T_A_X.

     AATT   Sets the volume for the footer for compatibility with man pages from
          AT&T UNIX releases.  The optional arguments specify which release it
          is from.  This macro is an extension that first appeared in 4.3BSD.

     BB    Text is rendered in bold face.

     BBII   Text is rendered alternately in bold face and italic.  Thus, ‘.BI
          this word and that’ causes ‘this’ and ‘and’ to render in bold face,
          while ‘word’ and ‘that’ render in italics.  Whitespace between argu‐
          ments is omitted in output.

          Example:

                .BI bold italic bold italic

     BBRR   Text is rendered alternately in bold face and roman (the default
          font).  Whitespace between arguments is omitted in output.  See also
          BBII.

     DDTT   Restore the default tabulator positions.  They are at intervals of
          0.5 inches.  This has no effect unless the tabulator positions were
          changed with the roff(7) ttaa request.

     EEEE   End an example block started with EEXX.  This is a Version 9 AT&T UNIX
          extension later adopted by GNU.  In mandoc(1), it does the same as
          the roff(7) ffii request (switch to fill mode).

     EEXX   Begin a block to display an example.  This is a Version 9 AT&T UNIX
          extension later adopted by GNU.  In mandoc(1), it does the same as
          the roff(7) nnff request (switch to no-fill mode).

     HHPP   Begin a paragraph whose initial output line is left-justified, but
          subsequent output lines are indented, with the following syntax:

                .HHPP [_w_i_d_t_h]

          The _w_i_d_t_h argument is a roff(7) scaling width.  If specified, it's
          saved for later paragraph left margins; if unspecified, the saved or
          default width is used.

     II    Text is rendered in italics.

     IIBB   Text is rendered alternately in italics and bold face.  Whitespace
          between arguments is omitted in output.  See also BBII.

     IIPP   Begin an indented paragraph with the following syntax:

                .IIPP [_h_e_a_d [_w_i_d_t_h]]

          The _w_i_d_t_h argument is a roff(7) scaling width defining the left mar‐
          gin.  It's saved for later paragraph left-margins; if unspecified,
          the saved or default width is used.

          The _h_e_a_d argument is used as a leading term, flushed to the left
          margin.  This is useful for bulleted paragraphs and so on.

     IIRR   Text is rendered alternately in italics and roman (the default
          font).  Whitespace between arguments is omitted in output.  See also
          BBII.

     LLPP   A synonym for PPPP.

     MMEE   End a mailto block started with MMTT.  This is a GNU extension.

     MMRR   Reference another manual page.  This is a Plan 9 extension also sup‐
          ported by GNU.  It has the following syntax:

                .MMRR _n_a_m_e _s_e_c_t_i_o_n [_s_u_f_f_i_x]

          The optional, single _s_u_f_f_i_x argument is appended without preceding
          whitespace and typically used for trailing punctuation.

     MMTT   Begin a mailto block.  This is a GNU extension.  It has the follow‐
          ing syntax:

                .MMTT _a_d_d_r_e_s_s
                link description to be shown
                .MMEE

     OOPP   Optional command-line argument.  This is a deprecated GNU extension.
          The name and purpose of the macro match an earlier DWB extension,
          but both the syntax and semantics are incompatible.  In GNU and
          mandoc(1), it has the following syntax:

                .OOPP _k_e_y [_v_a_l_u_e]

          The _k_e_y is usually a command-line flag and _v_a_l_u_e its argument.

     PP    This synonym for PPPP is an AT&T System III UNIX extension later
          adopted by 4.3BSD.

     PPDD   Specify the vertical space to be inserted before each new paragraph.
          The syntax is as follows:

                .PPDD [_h_e_i_g_h_t]

          The _h_e_i_g_h_t argument is a roff(7) scaling width.  It defaults to 11vv.
          If the unit is omitted, vv is assumed.

          This macro affects the spacing before any subsequent instances of
          HHPP, IIPP, LLPP, PP, PPPP, SSHH, SSSS, SSYY, and TTPP.

     PPPP   Begin an undecorated paragraph.  The scope of a paragraph is closed
          by a subsequent paragraph, sub-section, section, or end of file.
          The saved paragraph left-margin width is reset to the default.

     RRBB   Text is rendered alternately in roman (the default font) and bold
          face.  Whitespace between arguments is omitted in output.  See also
          BBII.

     RREE   Explicitly close out the scope of a prior RRSS.  The default left mar‐
          gin is restored to the state before that RRSS invocation.

          The syntax is as follows:

                .RREE [_l_e_v_e_l]

          Without an argument, the most recent RRSS block is closed out.  If
          _l_e_v_e_l is 1, all open RRSS blocks are closed out.  Otherwise, _l_e_v_e_l − 1
          nested RRSS blocks remain open.

     RRII   Text is rendered alternately in roman (the default font) and ital‐
          ics.  Whitespace between arguments is omitted in output.  See also
          BBII.

     RRSS   Temporarily reset the default left margin.  This has the following
          syntax:

                .RRSS [_w_i_d_t_h]

          The _w_i_d_t_h argument is a roff(7) scaling width.  If not specified,
          the saved or default width is used.

          See also RREE.

     SSBB   Text is rendered in small size (one point smaller than the default
          font) bold face.  This macro is an extension that probably first ap‐
          peared in SunOS 4.0 and was later adopted by GNU and by 4.4BSD.

     SSHH   Begin a section.  The scope of a section is only closed by another
          section or the end of file.  The paragraph left-margin width is re‐
          set to the default.

     SSMM   Text is rendered in small size (one point smaller than the default
          font).

     SSSS   Begin a sub-section.  The scope of a sub-section is closed by a sub‐
          sequent sub-section, section, or end of file.  The paragraph left-
          margin width is reset to the default.

     SSYY   Begin a synopsis block with the following syntax:

                .SSYY _c_o_m_m_a_n_d
                _a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t_s
                .YYSS

          This is a GNU extension and rarely used even in GNU manual pages.
          Formatting is similar to IIPP.

     TTHH   Set the name of the manual page for use in the page header and
          footer with the following syntax:

                .TTHH _n_a_m_e _s_e_c_t_i_o_n _d_a_t_e [_s_o_u_r_c_e [_v_o_l_u_m_e]]

          Conventionally, the document _n_a_m_e is given in all caps.  The _s_e_c_t_i_o_n
          is usually a single digit, in a few cases followed by a letter.  The
          recommended _d_a_t_e format is YYYYYYYY--MMMM--DDDD as specified in the ISO-8601
          standard; if the argument does not conform, it is printed verbatim.
          If the _d_a_t_e is empty or not specified, the current date is used.
          The optional _s_o_u_r_c_e string specifies the organisation providing the
          utility.  When unspecified, mandoc(1) uses its --IIooss argument.  The
          _v_o_l_u_m_e string replaces the default volume title of the _s_e_c_t_i_o_n.

          Examples:

                .TH CVS 5 1992-02-12 GNU

     TTPP   Begin a paragraph where the head, if exceeding the indentation
          width, is followed by a newline; if not, the body follows on the
          same line after advancing to the indentation width.  Subsequent out‐
          put lines are indented.  The syntax is as follows:

                .TTPP [_w_i_d_t_h]
                _h_e_a_d \" one line
                _b_o_d_y

          The _w_i_d_t_h argument is a roff(7) scaling width.  If specified, it's
          saved for later paragraph left-margins; if unspecified, the saved or
          default width is used.

     TTQQ   Like TTPP, except that no vertical spacing is inserted before the
          paragraph.  This is a GNU extension.

     UUCC   Sets the volume for the footer for compatibility with man pages from
          BSD releases.  The optional first argument specifies which release
          it is from.  This macro is an extension that first appeared in 3BSD.

     UUEE   End a uniform resource identifier block started with UURR.  This is a
          GNU extension.

     UURR   Begin a uniform resource identifier block.  This is a GNU extension.
          It has the following syntax:

                .UURR _u_r_i
                link description to be shown
                .UUEE

     YYSS   End a synopsis block started with SSYY.  This is a GNU extension.

     iinn   Indent relative to the current indentation:

                .iinn [_w_i_d_t_h]

          If _w_i_d_t_h is signed, the new offset is relative.  Otherwise, it is
          absolute.  This value is reset upon the next paragraph, section, or
          sub-section.

MMAACCRROO SSYYNNTTAAXX
     The mmaann macros are classified by scope: line scope or block scope.  Line
     macros are only scoped to the current line (and, in some situations, the
     subsequent line).  Block macros are scoped to the current line and subse‐
     quent lines until closed by another block macro.

   LLiinnee MMaaccrrooss
     Line macros are generally scoped to the current line, with the body con‐
     sisting of zero or more arguments.  If a macro is scoped to the next line
     and the line arguments are empty, the next line, which must be text, is
     used instead.  Thus:

           .I
           foo

     is equivalent to ‘.I foo’.  If next-line macros are invoked consecu‐
     tively, only the last is used.  If a next-line macro is followed by a
     non-next-line macro, an error is raised.

     The syntax is as follows:

           .\" current-line syntax
           .YO [body ...]

           .\" next-line syntax
           .YO
           body ...

           _M_a_c_r_o    _A_r_g_u_m_e_n_t_s    _L_i_n_e _S_c_o_p_e       _N_o_t_e_s
           AATT       0 to 2       current
           BB        1 or more    curr or next
           BBII       2 or more    current
           BBRR       2 or more    current
           DDTT       0            none
           EEEE       0            none             Version 9 AT&T UNIX
           EEXX       0            none             Version 9 AT&T UNIX
           II        1 or more    curr or next
           IIBB       2 or more    current
           IIRR       2 or more    current
           MMRR       2 or 3       current          Plan 9
           OOPP       1 or 2       current          GNU
           PPDD       0 or 1       current
           RRBB       2 or more    current
           RRII       2 or more    current
           SSBB       1 or more    curr or next
           SSMM       1 or more    curr or next
           TTHH       3 to 5       current
           UUCC       0 or 1       current
           iinn       0 or 1       current          roff(7)

   BBlloocckk MMaaccrrooss
     Block macros comprise a head and body.  As with in-line macros, the head
     is scoped to the current line or, for some macros, to the next line (the
     next-line stipulations as in _L_i_n_e _M_a_c_r_o_s apply here as well).

     The syntax is as follows:

           .\" current-line syntax
           .YO [head ...]
           body ...
           ...

           .\" next-line syntax
           .YO [head]
           head ...
           body ...
           ...

     The closure of body scope may be to the section, where a macro is closed
     by SSHH; sub-section, closed by a section or SSSS; paragraph, closed by a
     section, sub-section, HHPP, IIPP, LLPP, PP, PPPP, RRSS, SSYY, TTPP, or TTQQ; or to an ex‐
     plicit block closing macro.

     As a rule, block macros may not be nested; thus, calling a block macro
     while another block macro scope is open, and the open scope is not im‐
     plicitly closed, is syntactically incorrect.

           _M_a_c_r_o   _A_r_g_u_m_e_n_t_s   _H_e_a_d _S_c_o_p_e      _B_o_d_y _S_c_o_p_e    _N_o_t_e_s
           HHPP      0 or 1      current         paragraph
           IIPP      0 to 2      current         paragraph
           LLPP      0           none            paragraph
           MMEE      0 or 1      current         none          GNU
           MMTT      1           current         to ME         GNU
           PP       0           none            paragraph
           PPPP      0           none            paragraph
           RREE      0 or 1      current         none
           RRSS      0 or 1      current         to RE
           SSHH      1 or more   curr or next    section
           SSSS      1 or more   curr or next    sub-section
           SSYY      1           current         to YS         GNU
           TTPP      0 or 1      curr and next   paragraph
           TTQQ      0 or 1      curr and next   paragraph     GNU
           UUEE      0 or 1      current         none          GNU
           UURR      1           current         to UE         GNU
           YYSS      0           none            none          GNU

     If a block macro is next-line scoped, it may only be followed by in-line
     macros for decorating text.

   FFoonntt hhaannddlliinngg
     In mmaann documents, both _P_h_y_s_i_c_a_l _m_a_r_k_u_p macros and roff(7) ‘\f’ font es‐
     cape sequences can be used to choose fonts.  In text lines, the effect of
     manual font selection by escape sequences only lasts until the next macro
     invocation; in macro lines, it only lasts until the end of the macro
     scope.  Note that macros like BBRR open and close a font scope for each ar‐
     gument.

